\section{The four criteria}{

A period is classified by the first of these that fires. Criteria 1 and 2 are
definitive and give \code{"failed"}; criteria 3 and 4 are circumstantial and give
\code{"suspect"}. Anything untouched is \code{"converged"}.
\enumerate{
\item \strong{The period is not an optimisation.}
\code{optimize.portfolio.rebalancing()} runs its periods through
\code{foreach(..., .errorhandling = "pass")}, so a period whose solver throws
leaves a \strong{condition object} in \code{opt_rebalancing} rather than an
\code{optimize.portfolio}. Nothing warns, and downstream extractors do not
check. Test: \code{!inherits(x, "optimize.portfolio")}. Report
\code{conditionMessage(x)}.
\item \strong{The weights or the objective are not finite.}
On an infeasible or non-positive-definite problem ROI's \code{quadprog} plugin
returns a solution of \code{NA}s with a non-zero status code, and
\code{PortfolioAnalytics:::gmv_opt()} copies \code{result$solution} into \verb{$weights}
without looking at \code{result$status}. The period then carries \code{weights} of
\code{NA} and \code{out} of \code{NA}, silently, and \code{extractWeights()} returns a matrix of
\code{NA}. Verified against \code{PortfolioAnalytics} 2.1.2. Test:
\code{any(!is.finite(x$weights))} or \code{!is.finite(x$out)}.

Criteria 1 and 2 are two routes to the same place, and which one an
infeasible problem takes depends on the ROI plugin that resolves it: a
plugin that errors gives criterion 1, one that returns \code{NA}s gives
criterion 2. Both were observed for the same specification on the same
machine. An implementation must handle both, and the test file pins the
upstream behaviour accordingly.
\item \strong{The weights violate the specification.}
Read \code{min_sum} and \code{max_sum} from the \code{weight_sum} (or \code{full_investment})
constraint of \code{x$portfolio$constraints}, and \code{min} and \code{max} from the \code{box}
(or \code{long_only}) constraint. Flag when \code{sum(weights)} falls outside
\verb{[min_sum - tol, max_sum + tol]}, or any weight falls outside its box bound
by more than \code{tol}. A converged solve satisfies its own constraints; one
that does not was not a solve.
\item \strong{The weights are a degenerate fallback.}
Flag when the weight vector equals, to within \code{tol}, any of: the
equal-weight vector \code{rep(1/N, N)}; the \code{weight_initial} of a turnover
constraint, if there is one; or the previous period's weights, when the
estimation window has moved and the moments therefore have not repeated.
Several backends return their seed when they give up, and a metaheuristic
returns the best of a population that may never have improved on it.

The last of the three carries the qualification in its own wording, and it
is enforced: repeated weights are only flagged when the objective value
\strong{also} moved. If both the weights and the objective are unchanged, the
solver was handed the same problem twice and gave the same answer twice,
which is correct behaviour rather than a fallback. This is not hypothetical.
A historical expected shortfall at 95\% over 60 monthly observations is the
mean of the three worst months; roll the window by one month and, unless the
dropped or added month is one of those three, the linear programme is
literally identical. On the book's 16 risky class series, 243 of 313
minimum-CVaR periods repeat exactly, objective included — a fact about
tail estimation on short samples, not about the solver.
}
}

\section{What cannot be checked, and why criteria 3 and 4 exist}{

There is no solver status code on the returned object. \code{ROI::ROI_solve()}
reports one in \code{result$status$code}, and \code{PortfolioAnalytics} 2.1.2 discards it
— \code{gmv_opt()} and its siblings catch only a \code{try-error} and read
\code{result$solution} regardless of status. \code{DEoptim} and \code{random} backends store
no convergence flag either. So a non-converged solve cannot be detected
directly; it has to be caught by its symptoms, which is what criteria 3 and 4
are. This is the substance of issue #45 and the reason this function exists
rather than a one-line accessor.
}

\section{Deliberate weighting schemes}{

Criterion 4 asks whether the weights look like a seed the solver never
improved on. For \code{\link[PortfolioAnalytics:equal.weight]{PortfolioAnalytics::equal.weight()}} and
\code{\link[PortfolioAnalytics:inverse.volatility.weight]{PortfolioAnalytics::inverse.volatility.weight()}} the seed \emph{is} the answer:
they evaluate a rule, they do not search. Periods whose class is
\code{optimize.portfolio.eqwt} or \code{optimize.portfolio.invol} are therefore exempt
from criterion 4 — otherwise every period of a 1/N benchmark would come back
\code{"suspect"} and the column would stop carrying information. Criteria 1 to 3
still apply to them: a rule can still throw, still return \code{NA}, and still
violate a box constraint the caller wrote.
}

\section{Reading the constraints}{

Criterion 3 keys on the constraint object's \strong{class}, not on its \verb{$type}.
\code{add.constraint(type = "full_investment")} returns a \code{weight_sum_constraint}
whose \verb{$type} is \code{"full_investment"} and which \emph{also} carries \verb{$min} and
\verb{$max} of 1; read as a box constraint it would appear to pin every weight to
\enumerate{
\item \code{long_only} and \code{box} both return a \code{box_constraint}, with \verb{$type} equal to
\code{"long_only"} and \code{"box"} respectively. Where several box constraints are
enabled the tightest bound of each is used, which is what the solver faces.
}
}

